---
description: DylKit app workflow — checks before a PR and where the conventions live
alwaysApply: true
---

Before opening or updating a PR, run `./run dev checks` (SwiftLint, `dyl-lint`, and `release-kit check`) and fix what it reports. Don't restate lint rules in code or docs; the findings explain themselves. Use `// lint:allow <rule>: <reason>` only for a verified benign hit.

Never take focus on Dylan's Mac. Launch apps only with `open -g`, which is all `./run dev relaunch` and `./run dev capture` do. Never use `osascript` (AppleScript `activate`, System Events keystrokes or menu clicks), `open` without `-g`, the app binary run directly, `open -a Simulator`, or a capture that raises a window. If a check needs any of those, it doesn't run on his Mac: say what's unverified. Never launch a build that shares the bundle ID of an app installed on his Mac, even with `open -g`. `build/<App>.app` carries the release bundle ID, so it writes to the installed app's preferences, Sparkle state and logs. Verify releases by comparing Info.plist, signatures and entitlements (`apple-dev-loop` has the commands), and launch only on simulators or under a separate test bundle ID. On his Mac, stay inside `~/repos/scone-os`: scratch worktrees go under its `build/worktrees/`, captures under `build/captures/`. Never write outside it: not `~/Desktop`, not `/tmp`. A capture Dylan should see goes in the Project store under `/cursor/stores/bc-3533d1ec-a627-4ef9-891d-baeea91e0ebb/media/`. The store isn't mounted on his Mac, so an agent running there leaves the capture in `build/captures/` and says where it is. These rules apply to prompts you write for other agents too.

DylKit apps are SwiftPM only, with no Xcode project, and use `@Observable` state only. `apple-dev-loop` is the one guide to setup, building, testing, launching, screenshots and the iOS Simulator, and `apple-release` covers releasing, in CI and by hand. Link them; don't copy their steps into READMEs. Other conventions live in the skills under `.cursor/skills`.
